  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       HOW IS SOCIAL SECURITY TAXED IN 2026?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How Is Social Security Taxed in 2026?</h2>
    <p>
      For many years, Social Security benefits were completely tax-free. However, since landmark legislation in 1983 and 1993, federal law requires a portion of Social Security benefits to be included in taxable income when a recipient has other financial resources.
    </p>
    <p>
      The critical rule is that <strong>Social Security is never 100% taxable at the federal level</strong>. Under the statutory IRS formula in <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/p915.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link">IRS Publication 915</a>, the maximum portion of your benefits that can ever be subject to federal income tax is <strong>85%</strong>. The remaining 15% is always federally tax-free.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       HOW THE CALCULATOR WORKS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section">
    <h2>How the Social Security Tax Calculator Works</h2>
    <p>
      Our calculation engine follows the exact seven-step methodology prescribed in the IRS Form 1040 Social Security Benefits Worksheet:
    </p>
    <ol class="sstc-numbered-list">
      <li><strong>Calculates 50% of your annual Social Security benefits:</strong> Half of your total benefit check forms the foundation of your taxability test.</li>
      <li><strong>Adds other taxable income:</strong> Sums your pensions, traditional IRA and 401(k) distributions, wages, interest, dividends, and rental earnings.</li>
      <li><strong>Adds tax-exempt interest:</strong> Municipal bond interest is included per statutory IRS rules.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculates combined/provisional income:</strong> The resulting sum is evaluated against statutory thresholds for your filing status.</li>
      <li><strong>Determines taxable Social Security:</strong> Applies the exact two-tier IRS formula (Tier 1 up to 50%, Tier 2 up to 85%).</li>
      <li><strong>Calculates estimated taxable income:</strong> Adds taxable Social Security to other income and subtracts your 2026 standard deduction (including age 65+ senior enhancements).</li>
      <li><strong>Estimates federal tax and withholding balance:</strong> Evaluates taxable income across official 2026 IRS tax brackets (<a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link">IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32</a>) and compares against voluntary withholding.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       WHAT IS COMBINED INCOME?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="combined-income">
    <h2>What Is Combined Income for Social Security Taxes?</h2>
    <p>
      The IRS does not look at your Social Security benefits in isolation. Instead, it tests taxability using a metric known formally as <strong>Provisional Income</strong> or <strong>Combined Income</strong>:
    </p>

    <div class="sstc-formula-callout">
      <strong>Combined Income</strong> = Adjusted Gross Income (excluding Social Security) + Tax-Exempt Interest + 50% of Annual Social Security Benefits
    </div>

    <div class="sstc-grid-2">
      <div class="sstc-card-plain">
        <h3>What Is Included in Combined Income</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Wages, salaries, and net self-employment income</li>
          <li>Employer defined-benefit pensions</li>
          <li>Traditional IRA and 401(k) distributions</li>
          <li>Taxable interest and ordinary dividends</li>
          <li>Capital gains from taxable brokerage sales</li>
          <li>Tax-exempt municipal bond interest</li>
          <li><strong>50% of your gross Social Security benefits</strong></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="sstc-card-plain">
        <h3>What Is Excluded from Combined Income</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Qualified withdrawals from Roth IRAs and Roth 401(k)s</li>
          <li>Qualified Charitable Distributions (QCDs) from IRAs</li>
          <li>The remaining 50% of your Social Security benefits</li>
          <li>Non-taxable Veterans Affairs (VA) disability benefits</li>
          <li>Supplemental Security Income (SSI) payments</li>
          <li>Life insurance death benefit proceeds</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       HOW MUCH IS TAXABLE (THRESHOLDS TABLE)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="taxability-thresholds">
    <h2>How Much of Social Security Is Taxable? (2026 Thresholds)</h2>
    <p>
      The IRS establishes three distinct taxability zones based on your filing status and combined income. Because Congress never indexed these thresholds to inflation when creating them in 1983 and 1993, routine COLA increases and modest pensions push more seniors into taxable territory each year:
    </p>

    <div class="sstc-table-wrap">
      <table class="sstc-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Tax Filing Status</th>
            <th>0% Taxable (100% Tax-Free)</th>
            <th>Up to 50% Taxable</th>
            <th>Up to 85% Taxable</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Single / Head of Household</strong></td>
            <td>Combined Income under $25,000</td>
            <td>$25,000 – $34,000</td>
            <td>Combined Income over $34,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Married Filing Jointly</strong></td>
            <td>Combined Income under $32,000</td>
            <td>$32,000 – $44,000</td>
            <td>Combined Income over $44,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Married Filing Separately (lived apart all year)</strong></td>
            <td>Combined Income under $25,000</td>
            <td>$25,000 – $34,000</td>
            <td>Combined Income over $34,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Married Filing Separately (Lived together at any time)</strong></td>
            <td>$0 (No exemption)</td>
            <td>N/A</td>
            <td>Up to 85% taxable from the first dollar ($1)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="sstc-caption">
      Source: <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/p915.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link">IRS Publication 915 (Rev. 2025)</a>. Thresholds are fixed by statutory law.
    </p>

    <!-- Short 2027 Changes Box -->
    <div class="sstc-callout-note" style="background: #F0F7F6; border-left: 4px solid #0A3D3A; padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #0A3D3A; font-size: 1.15rem;">Upcoming 2027 Social Security Changes</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
        On October 14, 2026, the Social Security Administration announced official adjustments taking effect for 2027:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li><strong>Cost-of-Living Adjustment (COLA):</strong> 3.6% benefit increase starting January 2027.</li>
        <li><strong>Maximum Taxable Earnings:</strong> $192,300 subject to Social Security payroll tax (up from $184,500 in 2026).</li>
        <li><strong>Retirement Earnings Test (Under Full Retirement Age):</strong> $25,560 limit per year ($1 withheld per $2 earned above limit).</li>
        <li><strong>Retirement Earnings Test (Year Reaching FRA):</strong> $67,920 limit per year ($1 withheld per $3 earned above limit).</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: #4A5568;">
        Source: <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/cola/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link">Social Security Administration (SSA.gov COLA)</a>. Note that statutory federal taxability thresholds ($25,000 single / $32,000 joint) remain unchanged under IRC § 86.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       WHY 85% TAXABLE != 85% TAX RATE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="why-85-percent">
    <h2>Why Can Up to 85% of Social Security Be Taxable?</h2>
    <div class="sstc-callout-warning">
      <h3>Key Rule: 85% Taxable Does NOT Mean an 85% Tax Rate</h3>
      <p>
        One of the most frequent misunderstandings among retirees is confusing the <strong>taxable inclusion percentage</strong> with the <strong>tax rate</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the calculator indicates that 85% of your $24,000 Social Security benefit is taxable ($20,400), you do <strong>not</strong> pay 85% of that money to the government. Instead, $20,400 is added to your other income on Line 6b of Form 1040. After subtracting your standard deduction ($18,150 for a single senior in 2026), that income is taxed at ordinary federal rates — typically <strong>10% or 12%</strong>. Your actual effective tax rate is usually just 2% to 8%.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DOES IRA / PENSION INCOME MAKE SS TAXABLE?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="ira-income-impact">
    <h2>Does IRA or 401(k) Income Make Social Security Taxable?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. When you withdraw money from a Traditional IRA, 401(k), or 403(b), every dollar distributed counts as ordinary taxable income. This increases your Adjusted Gross Income, which directly increases your Combined Income.
    </p>
    <p>
      This interaction creates what tax planners call the <strong>Social Security "Tax Torpedo"</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="sstc-card-highlight">
      <h4>How the Tax Torpedo Works in Practice</h4>
      <p>
        Suppose you are in the phase-in zone where your Social Security is partially taxable. If you withdraw an extra $1,000 from your Traditional IRA:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The $1,000 IRA withdrawal is directly taxable.</li>
        <li>The $1,000 also increases your combined income, causing an <strong>additional $850 of Social Security</strong> to become taxable.</li>
        <li>Your total federal taxable income increases by <strong>$1,850</strong> for every $1,000 withdrawn.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If your marginal bracket is 12%, you pay 12% on $1,850 = $222 in tax, creating an <strong>effective marginal tax rate of 22.2%</strong> on a 12% bracket! You can model the standalone tax impact on individual distributions using our <a href="/tools/ira-withdrawal-tax-calculator/">IRA Withdrawal Tax Calculator</a> and <a href="/tools/401k-withdrawal-tax-calculator/">401(k) Withdrawal Tax Calculator</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sstc-section" id="pension-income-impact">
    <h2>Does Pension Income Make Social Security Taxable?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. Defined-benefit pensions from private employers, unions, and most government entities are fully taxable as ordinary income. A monthly pension of $2,500 ($30,000 per year) alone exceeds the $25,000 single threshold, ensuring that a significant portion of any Social Security benefit received alongside the pension will be subject to federal taxation.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you receive a government pension from employment where you did not pay Social Security taxes, also be aware of the Windfall Elimination Provision (WEP) and Government Pension Offset (GPO), which may reduce your underlying benefit amount.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       HOW TO AVOID A LARGE TAX BILL
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="avoid-tax-bill">
    <h2>Can I Avoid Having a Large Tax Bill on Social Security?</h2>
    <p>
      While you cannot change the statutory IRS formulas, several legitimate financial planning strategies can minimize unexpected tax hits:
    </p>
    <div class="sstc-grid-2">
      <div class="sstc-strategy-card">
        <h3>1. Qualified Charitable Distributions (QCDs)</h3>
        <p>
          If you are age 70½ or older and donate to charity, transfer funds directly from your Traditional IRA to a 501(c)(3) charity via a QCD (up to $111,000 in 2026 under IRS Notice 2025-67; QCDs must be completed by December 31). A QCD satisfies your Required Minimum Distribution (RMD) but is <strong>100% excluded from your AGI</strong>, preventing it from making your Social Security taxable.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-strategy-card">
        <h3>2. Strategic Partial Roth Conversions</h3>
        <p>
          Convert Traditional IRA funds to a Roth IRA during the "tax valley" between retirement and starting Social Security (or age 73 RMDs). Qualified distributions from Roth accounts in future years are completely tax-free and do not count toward combined income. Model this with our <a href="/tools/roth-conversion-calculator/">Roth Conversion Calculator</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-strategy-card">
        <h3>3. Voluntary Federal Tax Withholding</h3>
        <p>
          File <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/fw4v.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link">IRS Form W-4V</a> with the Social Security Administration to have 7%, 10%, 12%, or 22% withheld from your monthly checks. This spreads your tax liability smoothly over 12 months and prevents penalties. You can calculate the exact monthly withholding needed with our <a href="/tools/social-security-tax-withholding-calculator/">Social Security Tax Withholding Calculator</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-strategy-card">
        <h3>4. Coordinate Annual Drawdown Timing</h3>
        <p>
          Alternating annual withdrawals between taxable accounts, tax-deferred accounts, and tax-free Roth accounts helps keep combined income below the $25,000 or $32,000 threshold in alternating years.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       WITHHOLDING GUIDE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="withholding-guide">
    <h2>Should I Withhold Taxes From Social Security?</h2>
    <p>
      Many retirees assume that because Social Security is taxable, the government automatically deducts taxes. <strong>This is not true</strong>. The Social Security Administration does not withhold a single penny for federal taxes unless you explicitly instruct them to do so.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you do not have taxes withheld and do not make quarterly estimated tax payments via IRS Form 1040-ES, you may face a large balance due in April plus IRS underpayment penalties under IRC Section 6654.
    </p>
    <div class="sstc-cta-box">
      <h3>Need to calculate the exact withholding amount from your Social Security check?</h3>
      <p>Use our dedicated tool to estimate monthly Form W-4V deductions (7%, 10%, 12%, or 22%) and check your net deposit after tax.</p>
      <a href="/tools/social-security-tax-withholding-calculator/" class="sstc-btn-teal">→ Go to Social Security Tax Withholding Calculator</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       REAL CALCULATION EXAMPLES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="real-examples">
    <h2>5 Realistic Social Security Tax Calculation Examples</h2>
    <p>
      Here is how the official IRS calculation works across five common retiree scenarios for the 2026 tax year. For 2025–2028, eligible filers age 65+ may also claim the senior deduction (up to $6,000 per person, $12,000 married filing jointly), reduced by 6% of MAGI over $75,000 ($150,000 joint). Inside the $75,000–$175,000 MAGI range ($150,000–$250,000 joint) the deduction phase-out adds about 6% × your marginal rate to the effective marginal rate. Below, each example displays federal tax both ways (without and with the senior deduction):
    </p>

    <!-- Example 1 -->
    <div class="sstc-example-card">
      <div class="sstc-example-header">
        <span class="sstc-ex-badge">Example 1</span>
        <h3>Single Retiree — Social Security As Only Income ($26,000 Benefit)</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="sstc-table-wrap">
        <table class="sstc-table-sm">
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Gross Social Security Benefit</td><td>$26,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Other Taxable Income</td><td>$0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>50% of Social Security Benefit</td><td>$13,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Combined / Provisional Income</strong></td><td><strong>$13,000</strong> (Under $25,000 threshold)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxable Portion of Social Security</td><td><strong>$0 (0% Taxable — 100% Tax-Free)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2026 Senior Standard Deduction (Age 65+)</td><td>($18,150)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Senior deduction (2025–2028)</td><td>($6,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Estimated Federal Income Tax</strong></td><td><strong>$0</strong> (without Senior Ded) / <strong>$0</strong> (with Senior Ded)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sstc-ex-note"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Seniors whose sole financial support is Social Security pay zero federal income tax on their benefits; both the standard deduction and senior deduction bring federal tax to $0.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Example 2 -->
    <div class="sstc-example-card">
      <div class="sstc-example-header">
        <span class="sstc-ex-badge">Example 2</span>
        <h3>Single Retiree — Social Security + Moderate Pension ($22,000 SS + $20,000 Pension)</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="sstc-table-wrap">
        <table class="sstc-table-sm">
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Gross Social Security Benefit</td><td>$22,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pension Income</td><td>$20,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>50% of Social Security Benefit</td><td>$11,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Combined / Provisional Income</strong></td><td><strong>$31,000</strong> ($20k + $11k)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxability Tier</td><td>50% Tier ($25,000 to $34,000 range)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxable Social Security (50% of excess over $25k)</td><td><strong>$3,000</strong> (13.6% of benefit)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Adjusted Gross Income (AGI)</td><td>$23,000 ($20k pension + $3k taxable SS)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2026 Senior Standard Deduction (Age 65+)</td><td>($18,150) ($16.1k base + $2.05k senior)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Federal Taxable Income (before Senior Ded)</td><td>$4,850 (taxed at 10%)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Senior deduction (2025–2028)</td><td>($6,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Estimated Federal Income Tax</strong></td><td><strong>$485</strong> (without Senior Ded) / <strong>$0</strong> (with Senior Ded)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sstc-ex-note"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Even though $3,000 of Social Security is taxable, the standard deduction plus the senior deduction brings federal tax to $0.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Example 3 -->
    <div class="sstc-example-card">
      <div class="sstc-example-header">
        <span class="sstc-ex-badge">Example 3</span>
        <h3>Single Retiree — Social Security + IRA RMD ($28,000 SS + $45,000 IRA)</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="sstc-table-wrap">
        <table class="sstc-table-sm">
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Gross Social Security Benefit</td><td>$28,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Traditional IRA Distributions</td><td>$45,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>50% of Social Security Benefit</td><td>$14,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Combined / Provisional Income</strong></td><td><strong>$59,000</strong> ($45k + $14k — exceeds $34,000 tier)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxable Social Security</td><td><strong>$23,800</strong> (Maximum 85% taxable)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Adjusted Gross Income (AGI)</td><td>$68,800 ($45k IRA + $23.8k taxable SS)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2026 Senior Standard Deduction (Age 65+)</td><td>($18,150) ($16.1k base + $2.05k senior)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Federal Taxable Income (before Senior Ded)</td><td>$50,650</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Senior deduction (2025–2028)</td><td>($6,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Estimated Federal Income Tax</strong></td><td><strong>$5,855</strong> (without Senior Ded) / <strong>$5,110</strong> (with Senior Ded)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sstc-ex-note"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Substantial IRA withdrawals push benefits into the full 85% taxable zone, but 15% ($4,200) remains permanently tax-free; the senior deduction reduces total tax from $5,855 to $5,110.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Example 4 -->
    <div class="sstc-example-card">
      <div class="sstc-example-header">
        <span class="sstc-ex-badge">Example 4</span>
        <h3>Single Retiree — Social Security + Pension + IRA ($24,000 SS + $18,000 Pension + $12,000 IRA)</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="sstc-table-wrap">
        <table class="sstc-table-sm">
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Gross Social Security Benefit</td><td>$24,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pension + IRA Income</td><td>$30,000 ($18k + $12k)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>50% of Social Security Benefit</td><td>$12,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Combined / Provisional Income</strong></td><td><strong>$42,000</strong> ($30k + $12k)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxable Social Security (Tier 1 $4,500 + Tier 2 $6,800)</td><td><strong>$11,300</strong> (47.1% taxable)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Adjusted Gross Income (AGI)</td><td>$41,300 ($30k + $11.3k)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2026 Senior Standard Deduction (Age 65+)</td><td>($18,150) ($16.1k base + $2.05k senior)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Federal Taxable Income (before Senior Ded)</td><td>$23,150</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Senior deduction (2025–2028)</td><td>($6,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Estimated Federal Income Tax</strong></td><td><strong>$2,530</strong> (without Senior Ded) / <strong>$1,810</strong> (with Senior Ded)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sstc-ex-note"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Mixing moderate pensions and IRA distributions triggers both the 50% and 85% tiers; the senior deduction lowers federal tax from $2,530 to $1,810.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Example 5 -->
    <div class="sstc-example-card">
      <div class="sstc-example-header">
        <span class="sstc-ex-badge">Example 5</span>
        <h3>Married Couple — Joint Social Security + Pension ($46,000 Combined SS + $35,000 Pension)</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="sstc-table-wrap">
        <table class="sstc-table-sm">
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Combined Social Security Benefits (Both Spouses)</td><td>$46,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pension Income</td><td>$35,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>50% of Combined Social Security</td><td>$23,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Combined / Provisional Income</strong></td><td><strong>$58,000</strong> ($35k + $23k)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxability Tier</td><td>Joint Tier 2 (Exceeds $44,000 threshold)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Taxable Social Security</td><td><strong>$17,900</strong> (38.9% of combined benefit)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Adjusted Gross Income (AGI)</td><td>$52,900 ($35k + $17.9k)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2026 Senior Standard Deduction (Both 65+)</td><td>($35,500) ($32.2k base + $3.3k seniors)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Federal Taxable Income (before Senior Ded)</td><td>$17,400 (taxed entirely in 10% bracket)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Senior deduction (2025–2028)</td><td>($12,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Estimated Federal Income Tax</strong></td><td><strong>$1,740</strong> (without Senior Ded) / <strong>$540</strong> (with Senior Ded) (couple deduction $12,000)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="sstc-ex-note"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> The $35,500 senior joint standard deduction shields most income, and the $12,000 couple senior deduction reduces federal tax to $540.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       COMMON COSTLY MISTAKES SECTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>6 Costly Mistakes Seniors Make With Social Security Taxes</h2>
    <p>
      We frequently see retirees make preventable tax errors simply because official government instructions use confusing terminology. Here are six mistakes to avoid:
    </p>

    <div class="sstc-pitfalls-grid">
      <div class="sstc-pitfall-card">
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-num">1</div>
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-body">
          <h3>Panicking Over the "85% Taxable" Rule</h3>
          <p>
            Many 80-year-olds see "85% taxable" on a statement and fear the IRS will confiscate 85% of their monthly check. In reality, 85% is the portion included in your overall taxable income on Form 1040. Once standard deductions are applied, the actual tax rate on that money is usually just 10% or 12%.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-pitfall-card">
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-num">2</div>
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-body">
          <h3>Assuming Social Security Automatically Withholds Tax</h3>
          <p>
            Unlike wages where an employer withholds taxes automatically, Social Security withholds <strong>zero federal tax</strong> unless you file <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/fw4v.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link">IRS Form W-4V</a>. If you have pension or IRA income, leaving withholding at zero can lead to a surprise bill and IRS penalties in April.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-pitfall-card">
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-num">3</div>
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-body">
          <h3>The Age 73 RMD "Double Tax Surge"</h3>
          <p>
            When retirees reach age 73 and must take mandatory Required Minimum Distributions from Traditional IRAs, that extra income frequently pushes them above the $25,000 or $32,000 threshold. Not only is the IRA distribution taxed, but it simultaneously makes their Social Security taxable for the first time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-pitfall-card">
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-num">4</div>
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-body">
          <h3>Filing "Married Filing Separately" While Living Together</h3>
          <p>
            If you are married and choose Married Filing Separately, the IRS sets your tax-free threshold at <strong>$0</strong> if you lived together at any time during the tax year. Up to 85% of your Social Security becomes taxable starting from your very first dollar of income.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-pitfall-card">
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-num">5</div>
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-body">
          <h3>Forgetting Municipal Bond Interest in the Formula</h3>
          <p>
            Many seniors buy municipal bonds thinking the interest is 100% tax-free. While exempt from regular federal tax, the IRS legally adds municipal bond interest to your combined income when determining whether your Social Security is taxable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-pitfall-card">
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-num">6</div>
        <div class="sstc-pitfall-body">
          <h3>Missing the Extra Age 65+ Senior Standard Deduction</h3>
          <p>
            The IRS gives seniors age 65 and older a higher standard deduction ($2,050 extra for single filers, $1,650 per qualifying spouse for married joint filers in 2026, per <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link">IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32</a>). Failing to claim this extra deduction means overpaying taxes on your Social Security.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PLAIN-ENGLISH GLOSSARY / USED TERMINOLOGIES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="glossary-terms">
    <h2>Plain-English Glossary: Social Security Tax Terms Explained for Seniors</h2>
    <p>
      Government tax forms can be intimidating. Here is what the official terms actually mean in everyday plain English:
    </p>

    <div class="sstc-glossary-grid">
      <div class="sstc-glossary-card">
        <span class="sstc-glossary-term">Combined Income (Provisional Income)</span>
        <p class="sstc-glossary-def">
          <strong>The official IRS test number.</strong> It is your other income (pension, IRA, wages, interest) plus half of your annual Social Security check. If this number is under $25,000 (single) or $32,000 (married), your Social Security is 100% tax-free.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-glossary-card">
        <span class="sstc-glossary-term">Form SSA-1099</span>
        <p class="sstc-glossary-def">
          <strong>Your annual Social Security tax statement.</strong> Mailed to you every January by the Social Security Administration. Box 5 shows your net benefits (Box 3 minus any repayments in Box 4) for the year, and Box 6 shows any federal income tax you had withheld.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-glossary-card">
        <span class="sstc-glossary-term">Form W-4V (Voluntary Withholding Request)</span>
        <p class="sstc-glossary-def">
          <strong>The form to set up monthly tax deductions.</strong> A simple one-page IRS form you mail to your local Social Security office to have 7%, 10%, 12%, or 22% taken out of your monthly check automatically so you never owe a lump sum in April.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-glossary-card">
        <span class="sstc-glossary-term">The "Tax Torpedo"</span>
        <p class="sstc-glossary-def">
          <strong>The phase-in tax spike.</strong> When your income sits between the 50% and 85% thresholds, withdrawing $1,000 from an IRA forces up to $850 of your Social Security to become taxable too. This creates an effective tax rate higher than your regular tax bracket.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-glossary-card">
        <span class="sstc-glossary-term">Qualified Charitable Distribution (QCD)</span>
        <p class="sstc-glossary-def">
          <strong>A tax-free gift from your IRA.</strong> If you are age 70½ or older, you can send money directly from your IRA to a charity (up to $111,000 in 2026 per IRS Notice 2025-67). It counts toward your mandatory yearly withdrawal without adding a single dollar to your taxable income.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-glossary-card">
        <span class="sstc-glossary-term">Effective Tax Rate vs. Marginal Tax Bracket</span>
        <p class="sstc-glossary-def">
          <strong>Your true tax percentage vs. your top bracket.</strong> Your marginal bracket (e.g., 12%) applies only to your highest dollar of income. Your effective tax rate is what you actually pay across your entire income — typically just 2% to 6% for most retirees.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DECISION HELPER SECTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sstc-section" id="decision-helper">
    <h2>What Should I Do Based on My Result?</h2>
    <p>Use this simple decision framework based on your calculation output:</p>

    <div class="sstc-decision-grid">
      <div class="sstc-decision-item">
        <h3>If $0 of Your Social Security Is Taxable</h3>
        <p>
          Your combined income is safely below $25,000 (single) or $32,000 (joint). You owe zero federal tax on Social Security. However, be cautious when taking one-time IRA distributions, selling stock, or doing Roth conversions, as any additional income could push you across the threshold. If you have not claimed yet, you can also <a href="/tools/social-security-benefits-calculator/">estimate your baseline Social Security benefit</a> at different claiming ages.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-decision-item">
        <h3>If Up to 50% Is Taxable</h3>
        <p>
          You are in the first phase-in tier. In this zone, each additional dollar of outside income causes an extra 50 cents of Social Security to become taxable. Check how close you are to the 85% tier threshold ($34,000 single / $44,000 joint) to avoid escalating into the higher bracket.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-decision-item">
        <h3>If Up to 85% Is Taxable</h3>
        <p>
          You have reached the maximum federal inclusion cap. Remember: you are not paying an 85% tax rate — 85% of your benefit is simply included in taxable income. Because you are already at the 85% ceiling, additional IRA withdrawals will <strong>not</strong> cause any more Social Security to become taxable.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="sstc-decision-item">
        <h3>If You Have $0 Withholding and a Balance Due</h3>
        <p>
          If your estimated federal tax is greater than $1,000 and you have no withholding, model the right deduction using our <a href="/tools/social-security-tax-withholding-calculator/">Social Security Tax Withholding Calculator</a> and submit <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/fw4v.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sstc-inline-link">IRS Form W-4V</a> to the Social Security Administration. Electing 7% or 10% withholding will eliminate surprise tax bills and protect you from IRS penalty assessments.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

